1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,874
New money should feel good.

2
00:00:01,874 --> 00:00:05,900
Mostly it feels like paperwork you're scared to get wrong.

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:07,150
A funder said yes.

4
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:09,510
The award letter is in your inbox.

5
00:00:09,510 --> 00:00:11,731
But the money came with strings.

6
00:00:11,731 --> 00:00:14,161
It can only pay for certain things.

7
00:00:14,161 --> 00:00:19,783
And now you have to record it and split it the right way before you spend a cent.

8
00:00:19,783 --> 00:00:20,963
So let's do that.

9
00:00:20,963 --> 00:00:26,586
We'll add a grant, then split its money across the funds it's allowed to pay for.

10
00:00:26,586 --> 00:00:32,000
And GrantPipe does the math, so you can't promise more than you actually have.

11
00:00:31,650 --> 00:00:33,610
This is where your grants live.

12
00:00:33,610 --> 00:00:35,760
To add one, you click "Add grant."

13
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:37,593
It's a short form, two steps.

14
00:00:37,593 --> 00:00:38,605
First, the name.

15
00:00:38,605 --> 00:00:42,462
Call it what your funder calls it, so you recognize it later.

16
00:00:42,462 --> 00:00:45,687
Then the funder, the people who gave you the money.

17
00:00:45,687 --> 00:00:48,152
You pick from funders you already have.

18
00:00:48,152 --> 00:00:51,504
This one came from the Greater Cincinnati Foundation.

19
00:00:51,504 --> 00:00:53,717
The amount: sixty thousand dollars.

20
00:00:53,717 --> 00:00:54,665
And the status.

21
00:00:54,665 --> 00:00:58,395
We'll set this one to "Awarded," because that's what it is.

22
00:00:58,395 --> 00:00:59,850
The funder approved it.

23
00:00:59,850 --> 00:01:05,034
GrantPipe even tells you the next move: set up the award details before you spend.

24
00:01:05,034 --> 00:01:08,449
Step two is the dates, when the grant starts and ends.

25
00:01:08,449 --> 00:01:09,650
Then you create it.

26
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:10,488
Here's the grant.

27
00:01:10,488 --> 00:01:14,680
And here are the four numbers that matter, right at the top.

28
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:18,034
Grant Amount is the whole award: sixty thousand.

29
00:01:18,034 --> 00:01:21,877
Allocated is how much you've assigned to a fund so far.

30
00:01:21,877 --> 00:01:22,995
Right now, zero.

31
00:01:22,995 --> 00:01:27,117
Unallocated is what's still loose, the full sixty thousand.

32
00:01:27,117 --> 00:01:32,428
And Remaining to Spend is what's left after you pay bills against the grant.

33
00:01:32,428 --> 00:01:36,550
You haven't spent a cent yet, so that's sixty thousand too.

34
00:01:36,550 --> 00:01:41,092
So the money is in, but you haven't told GrantPipe what it's for.

35
00:01:41,092 --> 00:01:42,140
Let's fix that.

36
00:01:41,790 --> 00:01:44,343
A grant isn't one bucket of money.

37
00:01:44,343 --> 00:01:48,549
This one pays for two things, so it goes into two funds.

38
00:01:48,549 --> 00:01:51,177
You do that on the Allocations tab.

39
00:01:51,177 --> 00:01:55,232
First fund: Capacity Building, forty thousand dollars.

40
00:01:55,232 --> 00:02:00,339
You're saying, of this award, forty thousand pays for capacity work.

41
00:02:00,339 --> 00:02:03,042
Save it, and watch the numbers move.

42
00:02:03,042 --> 00:02:05,445
Allocated is now forty thousand.

43
00:02:05,445 --> 00:02:07,698
Unallocated dropped to twenty.

44
00:02:07,698 --> 00:02:11,002
You're assigning the money where it belongs.

45
00:02:11,002 --> 00:02:15,283
Second fund: General Operating, the last twenty thousand.

46
00:02:15,283 --> 00:02:17,235
And Unallocated hits zero.

47
00:02:17,235 --> 00:02:20,390
Every dollar of this grant now has a home.

48
00:02:20,390 --> 00:02:24,670
The math checks out, and you didn't do any of it by hand.

49
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:26,254
Now the part I'm proud of.

50
00:02:26,254 --> 00:02:30,048
Say you try to assign more than the grant is worth.

51
00:02:30,048 --> 00:02:32,205
A typo, or one fund too many.

52
00:02:32,205 --> 00:02:33,693
GrantPipe stops you.

53
00:02:33,693 --> 00:02:36,594
"Allocation would exceed grant amount."

54
00:02:36,594 --> 00:02:42,695
This is what makes restricted money scary, and it's the whole reason a tool helps.

55
00:02:42,695 --> 00:02:48,571
You can't promise sixty-five thousand dollars of a sixty-thousand-dollar grant.

56
00:02:48,571 --> 00:02:53,481
When an auditor asks if your allocations add up, the answer is yes

57
00:02:53,481 --> 00:02:56,680
because the software won't let you go over.

58
00:02:56,330 --> 00:03:01,473
That's a grant recorded and split across its funds, with the math handled for you.

59
00:03:01,473 --> 00:03:04,483
Two minutes, no spreadsheet, no second-guessing.

60
00:03:04,483 --> 00:03:05,800
Not in GrantPipe yet?

61
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:08,560
Maybe you're tracking this in a spreadsheet.

62
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:14,079
We made a free one built for restricted funds: the Restricted Fund Tracking Spreadsheet.

63
00:03:14,079 --> 00:03:14,580
Want it?

64
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:16,336
We'll send it to your inbox.

65
00:03:16,336 --> 00:03:21,730
Next, we'll track what you actually spend against this grant, so you know what's left.
